# The OpenCL template's path decision, Windows 11 + RTX 3060, 2026-11-27
# (excerpt of the runner's 645-line log, which stays in .validation/).
#
# Three things are in here, in the order the runner does them:
#   1. build\self-test\opencl_path_test.exe - the gates between the host's queue
#      and the CPU loop, from src/PathDecision.h. It links no OpenCL and no OFX
#      header, so the same 29 checks run on a machine with no driver, no loader
#      and no SDK. The gates, the parsing (including two unrecognised words) and
#      the handover faults are all here.
#   2. the forced-CPU contract run: OFXPLUGIN_OPENCL_MODE=cpu makes PathDecision's
#      first gate answer CPU, the plug-in renders one frame on the loop and writes
#      that decision into the read-only Device parameter, and --label-contains
#      fails the run if the label does not carry it. That is the half the
#      template could not prove before: on a machine whose host offers no OpenCL
#      the CPU path is reached by accident, and here it is reached on purpose.
#   3. the counts: three host profiles at 118 checks each, the forced run at 94,
#      the path test 29, the device suite 38 - 515 in the template, 3,141 in the
#      tree.
#
# What it does not prove: that the kernel's pixels equal the CPU loop's. That is
# opencl_suite's question and it still needs the device; this log says nothing
# about it either way.
